1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,428
This is the proposed compact coax connection for our magnetron.

2
00:00:04,428 --> 00:00:07,609
The cable on the right comes from the matching box.

3
00:00:07,609 --> 00:00:12,410
It plugs into a bulkhead S M A socket on the outside end of the service tube.

4
00:00:12,410 --> 00:00:15,341
A second coax cable continues inside that tube.

5
00:00:15,341 --> 00:00:18,645
The two coolant hoses use separate passages below it.

6
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:27,500
The terminal plate is actual C A D geometry; the connector internals shown here are explanatory geometry, not a manufacturer's detailed model.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:32,172
Watch the external plug approach the fixed socket.

8
00:00:32,172 --> 00:00:35,217
The male center pin enters the female center contact.

9
00:00:35,217 --> 00:00:38,607
The outer metal contacts meet around the insulating region.

10
00:00:38,607 --> 00:00:43,088
The coupling nut then threads onto the socket body and retains the connection.

11
00:00:43,088 --> 00:00:46,305
We turn the coupling nut rather than twisting the cable.

12
00:00:46,305 --> 00:00:52,567
This animation separates alignment and tightening for clarity; the contact and thread details are simplified.

13
00:00:54,367 --> 00:00:56,924
The orange parts show the live center path.

14
00:00:56,924 --> 00:01:04,596
The external cable center connects to the male pin, then to the female contact, and onward to the internal coax center conductor.

15
00:01:04,596 --> 00:01:06,618
These are intentionally connected.

16
00:01:06,618 --> 00:01:12,089
There is no voltage isolation between the external coax center and the internal coax center.

17
00:01:12,089 --> 00:01:14,112
They form one R F electrical node.

18
00:01:14,112 --> 00:01:19,167
The isolation we need is between that center path and the surrounding grounded metal.

19
00:01:20,967 --> 00:01:23,634
The cream colored material is the dielectric.

20
00:01:23,634 --> 00:01:30,510
Inside the S M A connector, an insulating support such as P T F E separates the center contact from the outer shell.

21
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:36,319
Inside each coax cable, its dielectric separates the center conductor from the surrounding shield.

22
00:01:36,319 --> 00:01:39,698
The outer cable jacket is an additional protective layer.

23
00:01:39,698 --> 00:01:44,677
The metal retaining nut and washer are mounting hardware, not electrical insulators.

24
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:48,767
The socket shell is intentionally bonded to the metal terminal plate.

25
00:01:50,567 --> 00:01:55,464
At the rear of the bulkhead socket, the internal cable is terminated as a coax assembly.

26
00:01:55,464 --> 00:02:01,807
Its center conductor connects to the center contact, using the connector manufacturer's specified assembly method.

27
00:02:01,807 --> 00:02:06,760
Its shield connects to the connector body through the rear termination and crimp ferrule.

28
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:14,606
A correctly assembled commercial pigtail provides this connection; we do not need a loose bare wire hanging off the back of the S M A socket.

29
00:02:14,606 --> 00:02:17,500
The outer jacket ends behind the shield termination.

30
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:21,165
Now move to the magnetron head.

31
00:02:21,165 --> 00:02:25,016
These sectioned bodies come from the reconstructed source C A D.

32
00:02:25,016 --> 00:02:30,552
The copper head and attached spacer are live; the bottom body and service tube are grounded.

33
00:02:30,552 --> 00:02:32,778
The main P T F E ring separates them.

34
00:02:32,778 --> 00:02:36,328
The proposed coax shield bond is on the grounded structure.

35
00:02:36,328 --> 00:02:41,503
The center conductor continues with dielectric protection to a separate live terminal.

36
00:02:41,503 --> 00:02:44,271
Only the short terminal connection is exposed.

37
00:02:44,271 --> 00:02:47,159
The shield must not connect to the live cathode.

38
00:02:47,159 --> 00:02:54,500
This head termination is a concept overlay; its clamp, insulation length and clearances still require detailed R F design.

39
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,518
Follow the two paths.

40
00:02:57,518 --> 00:03:02,563
The orange center path carries the R F connection from the matching box to the cathode.

41
00:03:02,563 --> 00:03:09,116
The blue gray outer path joins the cable shields, connector shells and grounded structure as the intended return.

42
00:03:09,116 --> 00:03:16,191
Solid dielectric separates the coax conductors, and the head insulator separates the live mechanical assembly from ground.

43
00:03:16,191 --> 00:03:21,758
This is an explanation of connectivity, not a calculated electric field or an electrical rating.

44
00:03:21,758 --> 00:03:28,833
We still need to verify the actual connector, cable, bias voltage and ignition conditions against the matching box output.
